{"name":"Lunette","hex":"#d0c8c6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lunette","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small opening in a vaulted roof of a circular or crescent shape. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A crescent-shaped recess or void in the space above a window or door.  a lunette in the Thomas Jefferson building of the US Library of Congress"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lunette"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lunette","blocks":[{"text":"A lunette is a crescent- or half-moon–shaped or semicircular architectural space or feature, variously filled with sculpture, painted, glazed, filled with recessed masonry, or void. A lunette may also be segmental, and the arch may be an arc taken from an ellipse. A lunette window is commonly called a half-moon window, or a fanlight if its muntins diverge radially."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Porte_entr%C3%A9e_principale_Palais_Luxembourg_Paris_2.jpg/330px-Porte_entr%C3%A9e_principale_Palais_Luxembourg_Paris_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunette","wikidata":"Q942787"},{"title":"Lunette (liturgy)","blocks":[{"text":"A lunette, also referred to as a luna or lunula, is an liturgical item used by in the Catholic Church for the exposition of the Host."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Ostensoir_et_Saint-Sacrement.jpg/330px-Ostensoir_et_Saint-Sacrement.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunette_(liturgy)","wikidata":"Q1420765"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Rest-Pearce-Highsmith.jpeg/500px-Rest-Pearce-Highsmith.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Rest-Pearce-Highsmith.jpeg/1280px-Rest-Pearce-Highsmith.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Rest-Pearce-Highsmith.jpeg/500px-Rest-Pearce-Highsmith.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Charles Sprague Pearce, Rest (1896). Mural in a lunette in the  Thomas Jefferson Building of the Library of Congress, Washington, D.C.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARest-Pearce-Highsmith.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/VillaPetraia.gif/500px-VillaPetraia.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/VillaPetraia.gif/500px-VillaPetraia.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Villa La Petraia in lunette form by Giusto Utens","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVillaPetraia.gif"}],"sounds":{"lunette":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/En-us-lunette.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/a/a7/En-us-lunette.ogg/En-us-lunette.ogg.mp3"}]}}}